I'm sure you're all familiar with venmo  and the PayPal but have you ever heard  of Zell Zell was only launched in June  of 2017 but they've been putting PayPal  to shame in fact they even nearly grown  to half of PayPal size within just these  five years currently PayPal handles just  over 300 billion dollars worth of  payments every quarter meanwhile ezella  has already reached over 150 billion per  quarter something else to note is that  the average Zell transaction is worth a  lot more than the average PayPal  transaction last year PayPal completed a  total of a 19.3 billion transactions  which translated to 1.25 trillion  dollars this means that each transaction  was worth 65 on average meanwhile Zell  has completed over 5 billion  transactions within the past five years  which translates to nearly one and a  half trillion dollars this means that  each transaction was worth nearly 300  dollars on average so not only has there  been exploding in popularity but it  appears to be the app of choice for  larger payments at this rate and seems  like Zell overtaking PayPal is  inevitable but how is a new player able  to put an established giant to shame  one of the main reasons for the  popularity of Azalea is the Players  behind it Zella isn't just some startup  like stripe or a firm it's actually the  brainchild of the largest financial  institutions in the world this includes  Bank of America Capital One JP Morgan  Chase PNC Bank truest U.S bank and Wells  Fargo with such large names behind it  you might be thinking that the success  of Zelda is a no-brainer but that's  actually not the case in fact Zell was  not their first attempt at taking on  PayPal their first attempt was actually  a service called clear exchange which  came out in April of 2011. the project  was initially launched by Bank of  America JPMorgan Chase and the Wells  Fargo and it was based on the same  ideology as zala eliminating the  friction and cost associated with making  payments instead of having to use  account numbers and routing numbers you  could just use someone's email address  or phone number but despite the  promising pitch clear exchange it didn't  not translate all that well in the real  world one of the key characteristics  that makes PayPal and venmo so popular  is that they've become a verbs it's  common for people to say I can PayPal  you or I can venmo you but ain't no one  gonna be saying I can clear exchange you  and given that much of these  transactions take place amongst younger  individuals and social settings public  perception is critical aside from having  terrible branding clear exchange was  also rather clunky and slow it was  painfully evident that clear exchange  was not designed by a modern  organization to initiate transfers you  had to navigate the portals on these  bank's websites and the apps and if you  used any of these portals 10 years ago  you know how bad the UI was it wasn't  just the UI that was clunky either the  payments themselves were just as clunky  as it took up to five business days for  transactions to be completed do you know  what else takes up to five business days  to complete pretty ACH transfers it  appeared that these Banks had just  thrown together a platform that could  initiate ACH transfers using just  someone's email address or phone number  but nothing else about the service stuck  out something else that we should note  is that clear exchanger offered no real  benefit to most users one of their  largest selling features was that there  were no fees to send money but the  problem is that PayPal nor venmo charge  any fees to send money to friends or  family either so really there was no  reason to use clear exchange from the  average person's perspective venmo was  already popular amongst their friend  groups transfers took less than five  days to complete the UI was a magnitudes  better and as far as they were concerned  it was free so there was really no  incentive to make the jump to clear  exchange at first the banks thought that  the reason for this failure was a lack  of participating members so they worked  on growing the size of their network but  eventually they realized that what a  clear change needed wasn't more  fundamental features what it needed was  a facelift and that brings us to the  introduction of Zell  the first problem that cell addressed  was The Branding shortfall they changed  the name from a clear exchange to zelm  Zell was not only easier to pronounce  than venmo and the PayPal but it was  unique and memorable as it started with  the Z the old branding wasn't the only  thing that the banks Left Behind either  they also more or less ditched their  institutional business remember how we  discussed that clear exchange was free  well it wasn't just cost effective for  individual users it was also cost  effective for institutional users unlike  PayPal and venmo so the banks were very  much hoping that businesses and  governments would start using the  service and that this would prompt  consumers to use it as well but this is  rarely how things work out it's almost  always the other way around institutions  usually shift to what their consumers  want this is precisely why businesses  like PayPal visa and MasterCard are so  successful consumers want to use them in  the business says have to pay the price  clear exchange was looking to eliminate  the price for businesses but that  doesn't make the service any more  popular amongst consumers the banks  eventually realized this with Zella and  they pretty much ditched the services  Market altogether instead it Zella is  only intended for paying people that you  already know it and to trust in fact  they all disclaims any responsibility  for goods and services that are bought  through their system while this might  sound like a disadvantage this is what  actually allows Brazil's biggest selling  feature by keeping the checking and  verifications to a minimum Sal was able  to allow for instant transfers this  feature was actually launched under  clear exchange but Zella is what became  known for it this was really a zealous  Checkmate move because this was  something that PayPal simply couldn't  compete with at least not very easily  PayPal would essentially have to become  a bank if they wanted to allow for  instant transfers PayPal has very much  moved in this direction but they still  haven't been able to eliminate instant  transfer fees altogether currently they  charge a 1.75 for instant transfers  something else that makes it all  especially appealing is that you don't  really have to create an account to use  it virtually everyone in the US already  has a bank account with a result  participant so all you have to do is  enable the Zell future within your bank  account this also means that payments go  directly to your bank account so you  don't have to deal with the nominal sums  of money just to laying around in your  PayPal account to be honest none of  these are groundbreaking changes from  clear exchange but the facts don't lie  as a Zella is magnitudes more successful  than clear exchange it just goes to show  that how you're offering a service is  just as important if not more important  than what you're offering it itself the  banks didn't realize this a decade ago  but with Zelda they're more aware than  ever which has given PayPal and event  mode are run for their money  foreign  like the perfect option for money  transfers but like always there is  another side to the story as well for  one zele does not offer any sort of  consumer or Seller Protection which  makes it virtually useless for small  businesses and Merchants now we  shouldn't note that PayPal services  within this Velma aren't that great  either in fact it's actually terrible  and it seems like PayPal almost always  hides with this camera regardless of  whether this camera is a customer or a  merchant and this is why PayPal has a  trustpilot review of it just 1.3 but